  "body": "`document.body.innerHTML = \"<script>alert(1);</scr\" + \"ipt>\";` does not cause code to execute, which is a valuable property for reasons that include a defense against some forms of XSS.\r\n\r\n`document.body.innerHTML = '<iframe srcdoc=\"<script>alert(1);</scr\" + \"ipt>\"></iframe>';`, on the other hand, does cause code to execute. This makes perfect sense from one perspective, as the script is executing in the context of a new document where [scripting is enabled](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-n-script). From another perspective, through, it's strange, as that document was created via a fragment where scripting was not enabled.\r\n\r\nSince we more or less treat `srcdoc` as part of the parent document (and, indeed, it's content is entirely determined by the parent), I'd suggest that it might make sense to cascade the scripting flag into the newly created document. We might, for example, set a flag on the `HTMLIframeElement`, cascade it to frames created in that frame, and read it out when preparing script to determine whether to proceed.\r\n\r\nThis would address some attack vectors @arturjanc and team are concerned about in Google properties. I'm just now adding counters to Chrome, so I don't have any numbers to point to, but my suspicion is that `srcdoc` usage is low enough to make a change like this both possible and reasonable (and if not by default, then certainly as an opt-in).\r\n\r\n/cc @freddyb, @bzbarsky, @johnwilander, @teddink in the hopes that other vendors have opinions.",
